{"id":"neet-2026r-q086","pn":86,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Equilibrium","st":"Solubility product and solubility","q":"The correct order of solubility of the given salts in water at 298 K is\nSalt — Ksp at 298 K\nAgBr — 5.0 × 10⁻¹³\nZn(OH)₂ — 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁵\nHg₂Cl₂ — 1.3 × 10⁻¹⁸","o":["AgBr > Zn(OH)₂ > Hg₂Cl₂","Hg₂Cl₂ > AgBr > Zn(OH)₂","Zn(OH)₂ > AgBr > Hg₂Cl₂","Hg₂Cl₂ > Zn(OH)₂ > AgBr"],"c":2,"s":"AgBr(s) ⇌ Ag⁺(aq) + Br⁻(aq).\nKsp = 5 × 10⁻¹³ = S², so S = √(5 × 10⁻¹³) = 7.07 × 10⁻⁷ mol/L.\nZn(OH)₂(s) ⇌ Zn²⁺(aq) + 2OH⁻(aq).\nKsp = 10⁻¹⁵ = (S)(2S)² = 4S³, so S = 0.63 × 10⁻⁵ = 6.3 × 10⁻⁶ mol/L.\nHg₂Cl₂(s) ⇌ Hg₂²⁺(aq) + 2Cl⁻(aq).\nKsp = 1.3 × 10⁻¹⁸ = 4S³, so S = 0.69 × 10⁻⁶ = 6.9 × 10⁻⁷ mol/L.\nCorrect order of solubility: Zn(OH)₂ > AgBr > Hg₂Cl₂."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q048","pn":48,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Equilibrium","st":"Cation analysis groups","q":"Match List I with List II\nList-I (Ion) — List-II (Group Number in Cation Analysis)\nA. Co²⁺ — I. Group-I\nB. Mg²⁺ — II. Group-III\nC. Pb²⁺ — III. Group-IV\nD. Al³⁺ — IV. Group-VI\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","o":["A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV"],"c":1,"s":"Co²⁺ is in Group-IV.\nMg²⁺ is in Group-VI.\nPb²⁺ is in Group-I.\nAl³⁺ is in Group-III.\nSo A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q059","pn":59,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Equilibrium","st":"Ionisation of polyprotic acids","q":"Phosphoric acid ionizes in three steps with their ionization constant values Ka₁, Ka₂ and Ka₃, respectively, while K is the overall ionization constant. Which of the following statements are true?\nA. log K = log Ka₁ + log Ka₂ + log Ka₃\nB. H₃PO₄ is a stronger acid than H₂PO₄⁻ and HPO₄²⁻\nC. Ka₁ > Ka₂ > Ka₃\nD. Ka₁ = (Ka₃ + Ka₂)/2\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","o":["A and B only","A and C only","B, C and D only","A, B and C only"],"c":3,"s":"H₃PO₄ is a stronger acid than H₂PO₄⁻ and HPO₄²⁻.\nH₃PO₄(aq) ⇌ H⁺(aq) + H₂PO₄⁻(aq), Ka₁ = 7.5 × 10⁻³.\nH₂PO₄⁻(aq) ⇌ H⁺(aq) + HPO₄²⁻(aq), Ka₂ = 6.2 × 10⁻⁸.\nHPO₄²⁻(aq) ⇌ H⁺(aq) + PO₄³⁻(aq), Ka₃ = 1.7 × 10⁻¹².\nSo Ka₁ > Ka₂ > Ka₃.\nK = Ka₁ × Ka₂ × Ka₃, so log K = log Ka₁ + log Ka₂ + log Ka₃.\nAnswer: A, B and C only."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q073","pn":73,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Equilibrium","st":"Kp and Kc relation","q":"For the reaction A(g) ⇌ 2B(g), the backward reaction rate constant is higher than the forward reaction rate constant by a factor of 2500, at 1000 K.\n[Given : R = 0.0831 L atm mol⁻¹ K⁻¹]\nKP for the reaction at 1000 K is","o":["83.1","2.077 × 10⁵","0.033","0.021"],"c":2,"s":"Kc = k_f/k_b = 1/2500\nKp = Kc(RT)^Δn_g, where Δn_g = 2 – 1 = 1\n= (1/2500) × 0.0831 × 1000\n= 0.033"},
{"id":"neet-2025-q088","pn":88,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Equilibrium","st":"Le Chatelier's principle","q":"Higher yield of NO in N₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2NO(g) can be obtained at\n[ΔH of the reaction = +180.7 kJ mol⁻¹]\nA. Higher temperature\nB. Lower temperature\nC. Higher concentration of N₂\nD. Higher concentration of O₂\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","o":["A, D only","B, C only","B, C, D only","A, C, D only"],"c":3,"s":"Yield of the product generally depends on temperature, concentration of reactant(s) and product(s), and pressure.\nThis is an endothermic reaction (ΔH = +180.7 kJ mol⁻¹), so an increase in temperature shifts the equilibrium in the forward direction and increases the yield of NO.\nIncrease in concentration of reactants (N₂ and O₂) also shifts the equilibrium in the forward direction and increases the yield of NO.\nHence, (A), (C) and (D) only will increase the yield of NO."}
